.nextflow*
work/
# Written into the launch directory by a run. Harmless, but one was committed by
# accident because it is a dotfile and easy to miss in `git status`. Launching from
# a separate run directory rather than the repo avoids producing them here at all.
.params_*.json
data/
results/
.DS_Store
testing/
testing*
*.pyc
null/
.lineage/
sandbox
samplesheets
lint_*
*lint_results*
*lint_console*
# scratch files written for the agent to read (lint reports, PR reviews)
histo_*_review.txt
histo_*.txt
# local test imagery. Gigabytes; never committed. Phase 6 puts small fixtures in
# a separate test-data repository instead.
local_test_datasets/
# The real test fixture: ~43 MB of 2x2-tile exemplar001. Git keeps every version of
# a binary forever, so this goes in a separate test-data repository fetched at test
# time. Stub tests use the few-hundred-byte placeholders in tests/stub_data instead.
fixture/
# Scratch space: params files with absolute paths, diagnostic scripts, runbooks,
# tool inputs. See scratch/README.md, itself ignored.
#
# Named `scratch/` and not `local/`. This directory was called `local/`, and a bare
# `local/` here matched a directory of that name at ANY depth, which silently ignored
# the whole of `modules/local/`. Files already tracked there stayed tracked, so nothing
# looked wrong until a NEW module was added and `git add` refused it; the module then
# went missing from its own commit while the workflow including it committed fine.
#
# Anchoring to `/local/` fixed that, but the name was still wrong: `modules/local`,
# `subworkflows/local`, `local_test_datasets` and Nextflow's own `local` executor all
# use the word for different things. The rename removes the ambiguity rather than
# working around it. The leading slash is kept regardless.
/scratch/
.nf-test*
# uv's virtualenv for the unit tests. uv.lock IS committed: it is what makes a
# local run and CI resolve the same versions, and this is a test environment rather
# than a library, so pinning is the point.
.venv/
